{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0167yY6bLruAjWQoMwiMS314","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good, the first two passed. Let me wait for more results. Meanwhile, let me look at the sha2 and shake backends to proactively fix similar issues. The sha2 `gen_message_random` uses sha256_inc_finalize which takes byte lengths (not bit lengths), so that should be different from blake. And shake also uses byte-level APIs. Let me check if there's a similar issue with the SHA2 finalize call in the KAT transcript:\n\nLooking at the C SHA2 transcript finalize:\n```c\nshaX_inc_finalize(outbuf, ctx->s, final_block, 1);\n```\nThe last arg is `1` which is `datalen` for sha256_inc_finalize. Let me check what the Rust does:\n\nThe Rust SHA2 KAT transcript finalize at main.rs:368-373:\n```rust\nlet final_block = [0u8; SHAX_BLOCK_BYTES];\nshax_inc_finalize(&mut outbuf, &mut self.s, &final_block, 1);\n```\n\nThis passes `1` as `datalen`. But `sha256_inc_finalize` expects datalen in bytes (the C code passes 1 byte of padding-like data). Let me check the C sha2 finalize to understand:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":292,"cache_read_input_tokens":134593,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":292},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"40cff8f1-55c4-4312-944f-3a5007119e9f","uuid":"2e35e798-f313-410b-b99b-3010fc7e8853"}
