which loads 8 bytes at once:\n```rust\nfor i in 0..(r as usize / 8) {\n    s_inc[i] ^= load64(&m[idx + 8 * i..]);\n}\n```\n\nThese should be equivalent for aligned data. Both XOR the same bytes into the state.\n\nActually, wait - there's a subtle issue. The Rust code's `if s_inc[25] != 0` block sets `s_inc[25] = 0` AFTER the permutation, but the C code sets `s_inc[25] = 0` BEFORE the permutation (and s_inc[25] is also state[25] which is part of the Keccak state in the C version). Wait no, actually `s_inc` is 26 u64 values. The first 25 are the Keccak state, and `s_inc[25]` is the counter. The `KeccakF1600_StatePermute(s_inc)` in C passes the same pointer, but only touches s_inc[0..24]. So setting s_inc[25] = 0 before or after the permute doesn't matter since permute doesn't touch s_inc[25].\n\nBut in the Rust code, I see that `s_inc[25] = 0` is set AFTER the permute. In C, `s_inc[25] = 0` is set BEFORE the permute. Let me check: in C line 451: `s_inc[25] = 0;` then line 453: `KeccakF1600_StatePermute(s_inc);`. So C sets it before. In Rust: permute first, then set to 0. Since permute doesn't touch index 25, the order doesn't matter. OK.\n\nHmm, let me look at the `keccak_inc_finalize` more carefully. The C code:\n```c\ns_inc[s_inc[25] >> 3] ^= (uint64_t)p << (8 * (s_inc[25] & 0x07));\ns_inc[(r - 1) >> 3] ^= (uint64_t)128 << (8 * ((r - 1) & 0x07));\ns_inc[25] = 0;\n```\n\nNote: no permute call here! But the Rust code does call permute:\n```rust\nfn keccak_inc_finalize(s_inc: &mut [u64; 26], r: u32, p: u8) {\n    let pos = s_inc[25] as usize;\n    s_inc[pos >> 3] ^= (p as u64) << (8 * (pos & 0x07));\n    s_inc[(r as usize - 1) >> 3] ^= 128u64 << (8 * ((r as usize - 1) & 0x07));\n    KeccakF1600_StatePermute(...);\n    s_inc[25] = 0;\n}\n```\n\nThe Rust code calls `KeccakF1600_StatePermute` in finalize, but the C code does NOT. This is a major bug! The C code defers the permutation to the squeeze phase. Let me fix:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_detail