{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RpCCL2F4tAKYWWksw2KmLH","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a complete picture. Here are the findings:\n\n---\n\n## Bug Report: Differences between C and Rust Haraka implementations\n\n### BUG 1: `br_aes_ct64_interleave_in` -- completely different interleaving scheme\n\n**C version** (`haraka.c` lines 561-587): Performs a 2-step bit spread (shift-16, shift-8), then combines with shift of 8:\n```c\n// Only 2 spread steps: <<16 then <<8\nx0 |= (x0 << 16); x0 &= 0x0000FFFF0000FFFF;\nx0 |= (x0 << 8);  x0 &= 0x00FF00FF00FF00FF;\n// Combine with << 8\n*q0 = x0 | (x2 << 8);\n*q1 = x1 | (x3 << 8);\n```\n\n**Rust version** (`haraka_impl.rs` lines 784-838): Performs a 5-step bit spread (shift-16, shift-8, shift-4, shift-2, shift-1), then combines with shift of 1:\n```rust\n// 5 spread steps: <<16, <<8, <<4, <<2, <<1\nx0 |= x0 << 16; x0 &= 0x0000FFFF0000FFFF;\nx0 |= x0 << 8;  x0 &= 0x00FF00FF00FF00FF;\nx0 |= x0 << 4;  x0 &= 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F;  // EXTRA\nx0 |= x0 << 2;  x0 &= 0x3333333333333333;    // EXTRA\nx0 |= x0 << 1;  x0 &= 0x5555555555555555;    // EXTRA\n// Combine with << 1\n*q0 = x0 | (x2 << 1);\n*q1 = x1 | (x3 << 1);\n```\n\nThe Rust version has **3 extra bit-spreading steps** and uses `<< 1` instead of `<< 8` for the final combine. This is a full-bit interleave (every other bit), whereas the C version is a byte-level interleave (every other byte). These produce completely different bit layouts.\n\n### BUG 2: `br_aes_ct64_interleave_out` -- inverse is also different\n\n**C version** (`haraka.c` lines 590-610): Uses byte-level de-interleave:\n```c\nx0 = q0 & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FF;\nx2 = (q0 >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FF;\n// 2 collapse steps, then final >> 16\n```\n\n**Rust version** (`haraka_impl.rs` lines 840-888): Uses full bit-level de-interleave:\n```rust\nx0 = q0 & 0x5555555555555555;\nx2 = (q0 >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555;\n// 5 collapse steps (>>1, >>2, >>4, >>8, >>16)\n```\n\nThe Rust version has **3 extra de-spreading steps** and extracts with `>> 1` and mask `0x5555...` instead of `>> 8` and mask `0x00FF00FF...`.\n\n### Functions that are IDENTICAL (no bugs found):\n\n1. **`br_aes_ct64_bitslice_Sbox`** -- All operations, constants, and variable assignments are identical.\n2. **`shift_rows`** -- All masks and shifts are identical.\n3. **`mix_columns`** -- All operations are identical.\n4. **`add_round_key`** -- Identical.\n5. **`br_aes_ct64_ortho`** -- All SWAP macros, constants, and shift amounts are identical.\n6. **Mix states section in `haraka512_perm`** -- All 13 mask/shift operations are identical.\n7. **Round constants** (`haraka512_rc64`) -- Identical.\n\n### Root Cause Summary\n\nThe Rust `br_aes_ct64_interleave_in` and `br_aes_ct64_interleave_out` implement a **full bit-level interleave** (spreading bits to every-other-bit positions), while the C versions implement a **byte-level interleave** (spreading bytes to every-other-byte positions). The two functions are exact inverses of each other within their respective schemes, but the schemes themselves are different. This means the internal bitsliced representation used during AES rounds is different between C and Rust, which will produce different final output from `haraka512_perm`.\n\nThe fix should be to replace the Rust interleave functions with the simpler C byte-level versions, changing:\n- `interleave_in`: Remove the 3 extra spread steps (`<<4`/`<<2`/`<<1`) and change the final combine from `<< 1` to `<< 8`\n- `interleave_out`: Start with mask `0x00FF00FF00FF00FF` and `>> 8` instead of `0x5555...` and `>> 1`, and remove the 3 extra collapse steps"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af5d8c2b10dbc9d46 (use SendMessage with to: 'af5d8c2b10dbc9d46' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 40475\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 96679</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"40cff8f1-55c4-4312-944f-3a5007119e9f","uuid":"7166d891-c54c-450a-bc3c-ab4fac4e5