F0F0F0F0F0F;  // EXTRA\nx0 |= x0 << 2;  x0 &= 0x3333333333333333;    // EXTRA\nx0 |= x0 << 1;  x0 &= 0x5555555555555555;    // EXTRA\n// Combine with << 1\n*q0 = x0 | (x2 << 1);\n*q1 = x1 | (x3 << 1);\n```\n\nThe Rust version has **3 extra bit-spreading steps** and uses `<< 1` instead of `<< 8` for the final combine. This is a full-bit interleave (every other bit), whereas the C version is a byte-level interleave (every other byte). These produce completely different bit layouts.\n\n### BUG 2: `br_aes_ct64_interleave_out` -- inverse is also different\n\n**C version** (`haraka.c` lines 590-610): Uses byte-level de-interleave:\n```c\nx0 = q0 & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FF;\nx2 = (q0 >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FF;\n// 2 collapse steps, then final >> 16\n```\n\n**Rust version** (`haraka_impl.rs` lines 840-888): Uses full bit-level de-interleave:\n```rust\nx0 = q0 & 0x5555555555555555;\nx2 = (q0 >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555;\n// 5 collapse steps (>>1, >>2, >>4, >>8, >>16)\n```\n\nThe Rust version has **3 extra de-spreading steps** and extracts with `>> 1` and mask `0x5555...` instead of `>> 8` and mask `0x00FF00FF...`.\n\n### Functions that are IDENTICAL (no bugs found):\n\n1. **`br_aes_ct64_bitslice_Sbox`** -- All operations, constants, and variable assignments are identical.\n2. **`shift_rows`** -- All masks and shifts are identical.\n3. **`mix_columns`** -- All operations are identical.\n4. **`add_round_key`** -- Identical.\n5. **`br_aes_ct64_ortho`** -- All SWAP macros, constants, and shift amounts are identical.\n6. **Mix states section in `haraka512_perm`** -- All 13 mask/shift operations are identical.\n7. **Round constants** (`haraka512_rc64`) -- Identical.\n\n### Root Cause Summary\n\nThe Rust `br_aes_ct64_interleave_in` and `br_aes_ct64_interleave_out` implement a **full bit-level interleave** (spreading bits to every-other-bit positions), while the C versions implement a **byte-level interleave** (spreading bytes to every-other-byte positions). The two functions are exact in